    "faq": {
      "type": "etl-faq",
      "settings": {
        "anchor": "faq",
        "title_prefix": "Frequently asked",
        "title_italic": "questions.",
        "tint": true
      },
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should my teaching CV have a photo?",
            "answer": "<p>For Canada and the USA, no. Canada's Job Bank says a photo isn't the norm and can lower your chances, and the U.S. EEOC says employers shouldn't ask for one. Recruiters for international schools, especially in Asia and the Middle East, often suggest a professional photo; that is recruiter advice, not a rule, so follow each school's instructions.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long should a teacher CV be?",
            "answer": "<p>Canada's Job Bank recommends two pages. For international schools, Teacher Horizons advises no more than two sides for teachers and three for senior leaders.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who should my referees be?",
            "answer": "<p>The International Taskforce on Child Protection recommends three referees who supervised you, one from your current or most recent employer, at assistant principal level or above. Open \"to whom it may concern\" letters are not accepted.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need to explain gaps in my employment?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Safer recruitment guidance expects a complete, dated employment history with any gaps explained, and schools will ask about them.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why fill in an application form when I have sent my CV?",
            "answer": "<p>Because international safer recruitment guidance says a CV isn't accepted in place of an application form. Forms make sure nothing, such as a gap, is left out.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which police checks will schools ask for?",
            "answer": "<p>Where possible, schools check every country you've lived in for six months or more in the last ten years. If you've lived in the UK, COBIS recommends the ACRO International Child Protection Certificate.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will I have to teach a demo lesson?",
            "answer": "<p>Possibly. Many international interviews include a live or recorded demo lesson, and England's Department for Education says you may be asked to teach a lesson or a micro-teach.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the STAR method?",
            "answer": "<p>Situation, Task, Action, Result: a way to answer behavioural questions with a real example. Say \"I\", not \"we\", so the panel hears what you actually did.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
